<ToolPage
  kind="guide"
  title="2-2-3 vs 4-On-4-Off: Which 12-Hour Rotation Fits Your Life?"
  description="An honest comparison of the two most common 12-hour rotations — weekends, fatigue, childcare, overtime, and family life — with 12-month calendars you can generate for both."
  h1="2-2-3 vs 4-On-4-Off"
>
  <p>
    If your plant, hospital, or distribution center is voting on a 12-hour schedule, the ballot
    almost always comes down to these two. Both split the workforce into squads working half of all
    days; both average 42 hours a week. The lived experience, though, is completely different — and
    the difference comes down to one number: 14 versus 8.
  </p>

  <h2>The structural difference</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/2-2-3-panama-schedule-calculator">2-2-3</a> runs a 14-day cycle: 2 on, 2 off,
    3 on, 2 off, 2 on, 3 off. Because 14 divides evenly into the week, the pattern is
    <strong>anchored to weekdays forever</strong> — your Mondays stay Mondays, and every other
    weekend is guaranteed off, in full. The <a href="/4-on-4-off-schedule-calculator">4-on-4-off</a>
    runs an 8-day cycle that does not divide into 7, so it <strong>drifts</strong>: each block
    starts one weekday earlier than the last, sweeping your days off through the week roughly every
    eight weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Weekends: guaranteed halves vs roaming wholes</h2>
  <p>
    On a 2-2-3 you can promise your family every other weekend — permanently, no calendar required.
    That predictability is why it wins most workplace votes. On 4-on-4-off, some breaks contain a
    full Saturday–Sunday and some contain neither; over a year it evens out, but you cannot promise
    "every other weekend," only "weekends on a rota." The flip side: 4-on-4-off's breaks are four
    days deep. A 2-2-3 never gives you more than three days off without leave; 4-on-4-off hands you
    a four-day break every single cycle — long enough for real trips.
  </p>

  <h2>Fatigue</h2>
  <p>
    Shift-fatigue research consistently finds alertness dips on the third and fourth consecutive
    12-hour shift, especially nights. The 2-2-3 never schedules more than three in a row; 4-on-4-off
    schedules four every cycle. If your work is safety-critical night work, that fourth night is the
    schedule's real cost, and the four-day recovery that follows is what pays for it. People who
    sleep well recover fine; people juggling small children through day-sleep often do not.
  </p>

  <h2>Pay and overtime</h2>
  <p>
    Both average 42 hours, but the weekly shapes differ: 2-2-3 alternates 36- and 48-hour weeks,
    while 4-on-4-off produces its own alternating rhythm around the 40-hour FLSA line. In both
    cases, weeks over 40 hours owe overtime at your <em>regular rate</em> — which must include any
    shift differential you earn. Most payroll mistakes in 24/7 shops happen exactly here; check
    your own numbers with the <a href="/shift-differential-calculator">differential and overtime
    calculator</a> and the <a href="/12-hour-shift-yearly-hours-calculator">yearly hours
    calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Childcare and two-shift households</h2>
  <p>
    The 2-2-3's fixed weekday anchor makes recurring commitments (daycare contracts, weekly
    practices) far easier to schedule. The 4-on-4-off's drift is harder on recurring commitments
    but better for households where both partners rotate: opposite squads on 4-on-4-off overlap
    their off-days predictably every cycle, which many two-worker families use to cover childcare
    without daycare at all.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest verdict</h2>
  <p>
    Choose the <strong>2-2-3</strong> if weekend predictability and shorter fatigue runs matter
    most — it is the safer default for families with school-age kids. Choose
    <strong>4-on-4-off</strong> if deep breaks and simple block structure matter more, or if you
    share childcare with another shift worker. Before any vote, generate a full year of each with
    one known start date — <a href="/2-2-3-panama-schedule-calculator">2-2-3 here</a> and
    <a href="/4-on-4-off-schedule-calculator">4-on-4-off here</a> — and lay the two calendars side
    by side. The holiday answers alone usually end the argument.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
